.he "booting (8)                   11/4/26                    booting (8)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
booting
- how Micronix starts, and single and multi-user
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Micronix starts in four levels.  The monitor ROM in its socket
(level 0) loads the level 1 ROM; level 1 probes the disk controllers
and boots the first labelled medium it finds.  That medium's first
sector knows its own controller (level 2), and the filesystem loader
it starts reads /micronix and enters the kernel (level 3).
.sp
The kernel's first process is init, which owns the console and brings
the machine up.  It runs /etc/rc.single if that file exists - on the
console, before the shell - prints /etc/signon, and execs /bin/sh as a
login shell, with argv[0] beginning with a dash.  Root's home is /, so
the shell reads /.sh and, because it is a login shell, /.login.
.sp
A shell that has finished its login script and is waiting at the
console has no work in hand.  Init samples it once a second; when the
shell has been at that tty wait for thirty seconds with no command
going through it, init kills it and goes multi-user.  Printing on the
console marks both ends of the wait:
.sp
.nf
.in +4
init: single user ttywait; 30 seconds
init: single user idle; going multiuser
.in -4
.fi
.sp
A command after the wait is somebody using the machine: a live child
of the shell, or the shell leaving the tty wait at any sample, cancels
the automatic change for the rest of the session.  A machine that is
being used stays single user until the shell exits.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
MULTI USER
.in +5
.br
Init runs /etc/rc to completion, then reads /etc/ttys and starts one
login per enabled line; it respawns them as they end.  The shipped
/etc/rc checks every filesystem /etc/fstab names with fsck -a -y,
skipping the mounted root, and then starts update.
.sp
A failed check signals init, which returns to the single-user shell
instead of opening the logins, so a machine whose disk needs repair
comes up where it can be repaired.  Every later multiuser start runs
the same check, so the repairs are tested again before the logins.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
RETURNING TO SINGLE USER
.in +5
.br
Sending SIGINT to init - kill -INT 1 - asks for the single-user
shell.  Init kills every other process, runs /etc/rc.single and the
console shell again, and puts the logins back when that shell exits.
A session requested this way has no idle timer: it stays until
somebody types exit.  This is what fsck -a does when a check fails.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/etc/rc.single	work run before each single-user shell
.br
/etc/rc	multi-user start-up
.br
/etc/fstab	the filesystems fsck -a checks
.br
/etc/ttys	the lines init starts logins on
.br
/etc/signon	the message printed at power-up
.br
/.sh, /.login	read by the console login shell
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
init (1), fsck (1), mount (1), halt (1), boot (1)
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
BUGS
.in +5
.br
The wait is sampled once a second.  A command that begins and ends
between two samples is not seen, and the next sample finds the shell
at the tty again; the timeout is about the shell being idle, not a
record of everything typed.
.sp
The timer is the host's wall clock: the console's clock registers name
a real rate, so the thirty seconds are real seconds even though the
simulated processor runs much faster.
